[
    {
        "content": "<p>In <code>FirstOrder/Arithmetic/Basic/Misc.lean</code>, there is a definition of the notation <code>∀ x &lt;⁺ t, φ</code> in formula literal notation, which expands to <code>∀ x, x &lt; t + 1 → φ</code>. Is the reason that this is distinct from <code>∀ x ≤ t, φ</code> because some weak theories of arithmetic like <code>𝗥₀</code> fail to prove that <code>x &lt; t + 1 ↔ x ≤ t</code>?</p>",
        "id": 628825894,
        "sender_full_name": "C7X",
        "timestamp": 1790996818
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It is because our definition of bounded arithmetical formula and arithmetical hierarchy <code>ℬ[&lt;, ℒₒᵣ].Hierarchy</code> only admits <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q x &lt; t</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">Q</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&lt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6151em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span></span></span></span>  as bounded quantifiers. So <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q x \\le t</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">Q</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6151em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span></span></span></span> is technically not a bounded quantifier, so we write <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q x &lt; t + 1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">Q</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&lt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6984em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span> instead.</p>",
        "id": 628962650,
        "sender_full_name": "Palalansoukî",
        "timestamp": 1791078150
    }
]